The warning came from the OpenAI provider defaults in `apps/backend/src/agents/providers.ts`, which sent `reasoningSummary: 'auto'` on every call regardless of model. GPT-4.1 is a sampling model (`thinking: 'none'` in its capabilities), so the AI SDK warned that the option isn't supported.

Now the provider defaults are filtered by model capability before being merged:

```211:220:apps/backend/src/agents/providers.ts
/** Provider defaults apply to every model, so reasoning-only ones must be dropped for sampling models. */
function resolveDefaultOptions(provider: LlmProvider, modelId: string, defaultOptions: object): object {
	if (getModelCapabilities(provider, modelId)?.thinking !== 'none') {
		return defaultOptions;
	}
	const { reasoningSummary: _reasoningSummary, ...sampling } = defaultOptions as { reasoningSummary?: unknown };
	return sampling;
}
```

Reasoning models like `gpt-5.5` keep `reasoningSummary: 'auto'`, and the other defaults (`store`, `truncation`) are untouched for all models. Azure is unaffected because it has no known model list — every Azure deployment resolves to the custom capability profile where we can't tell whether the deployment hosts a reasoning model.

I added two cases to `apps/backend/tests/inference-options.test.ts` covering both branches, and `npm run lint` is clean. The backend suite has pre-existing failures unrelated to this change (native `better-sqlite3` load errors and two OIDC allowlist assertions).

The test failures I'm seeing are pre-existing issues unrelated to my changes — they're native module problems with better-sqlite3 and some OIDC allowlist tests. The inference options are working as expected, so I'm done here.
